tory\",\n                base_directory.to_string_lossy()\n            )\n        })?;\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Detect if the user accidentally supplied a path instead of a search pattern.\n///\n/// Without `--full-path`, fd matches patterns against file names, so any pattern\n/// containing a path separator can never match. Two cases are worth a friendly\n/// error rather than silent \"no results\":\n///\n/// 1. The pattern contains '/'. '/' is always a path separator (including on\n///    Windows) and has no regex meaning, so flagging it is safe and catches the\n///    common Linux/macOS mistake of pasting a full path as the pattern.\n/// 2. On Windows only, the pattern contains the native `\\` separator *and*\n///    names an existing directory on disk. We can't treat `\\` as a pure\n///    path-separator signal there because it is also the regex escape char,\n///    so valid regex patterns like `\\Ac` or `\\d+` must still run. Requiring\ntests/tests.rs:389:        \"[fd error]: The search pattern 'nonexistent/path' contains a path-separation character and will not lead to any search results.\",\ntests/tests.rs:395:        \"[fd error]: The search pattern 'one/two/three' contains a path-separation character and will not lead to any search results.\",\ntests/tests.rs:400:/// so a path-separation character in the pattern is expected and must not\ntests/tests.rs:2078:fn test_exec_with_separator() {\ntests/tests.rs:2470:fn test_custom_path_separator() {\ntests/tests.rs:2713:fn test_invalid_cwd() {","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V2z6F1WqFr5VuRowch9rDx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
